{
  "id": "Compound::DB01001 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Salbutamol, a well-known adrenergic beta-2 agonist, is linked to asthma through its inclusion in the class of adrenergic beta-2 agonists, which includes Pirbuterol, Orciprenaline, and Indacaterol. These compounds are all classified as organic cyclic compounds and are known to act as bronchodilators, facilitating the relaxation of smooth muscle in the airways. The evidence suggests that these agents may exert their therapeutic effects by modulating beta-adrenergic receptors, which are critical in the regulation of bronchoconstriction and airway resistance. The shared ancestry of Salbutamol and its derivatives within the adrenergic beta-2 agonist class provides a consistent biological framework for their potential use in treating asthma. While the exact mechanism of action may vary slightly among the different compounds, the common pathway of receptor activation and bronchodilation supports a unified role in asthma management. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of adrenergic receptor modulation in the pathophysiology of asthma, with Salbutamol serving as a representative of this class that may be repurposed for therapeutic use.",
